At last, after several years of attempts to win a roguelike, I have
this final victory.  It certainly is satisfying to succeed!

My samurai had a very good start.  After clearing a few levels (kiii!)
I got to an altar.  Using wands and scrolls of create monster which I
found quite commonly, I sacrificed until AO saw fit to give me
Greayswandir and the Wallet of Perseus.  After taking a long sword from
the rat king's cold dead fingers I dipped it in a fountain and had
Excalibur.  As soon as I got to Expert level in long swords, I
two-weaponed the two artifacts to deadly effect.

I advanced through the levels cautiously, carving through the minions I
encountered.  All in all, there was nothing that really scared me.  As
soon as I found an unidentified item, I'd drag it back to the mall and
have it identified by those greedy yet helpful merchants.  Nothing
special, but its always good to know which potions are safe to drink.

Sokoban was tricky but possible, but I was somewhat disappointed with
the reward.  It was a bag of holding, but there was a catch:  1 - it
was cursed, and 2 - it contained a loadstone.  I decided to leave it
where it was.

By a funny coincidence, I never got to pay a priest for protection
because the one priest I met in the dungeon was massacred by my
god-given ki-rin before my eyes.

By now I was quite well off.  I had several stashes placed throughout
the dungeon.  I had tackled the mines, Soko, and the new slash'em
levels that i encountered - moloch's shrine, the largest giant,
shelob's lair, grund, etc.  I even found a portal to ludios, making me
rich.  70k zorkmids can make even a dour samurai happy.

The money went into ids, enchantments and proofing for my gear, and
(unfortunately) the occasional succubus lapdance.

My hitpoints soared too.  I love the beginning of the game, where every
battle can be intense simply because you die so easily, but it's nice
to be more robust!

Grund's stronghold was fun, if only because I picked up a submachine
gun and ammo.  Guns are a great feature.  Grenades, too.

The quest went as well as can be expected.  Down I go, and promptly
fall into a water square due to a typo.  Aaaah!  All my scrolls, which
I thought were safe inside the wallet, were ruined.  Except, that is,
for one blessed scroll of genocide that I was keeping for emergencies.
Deciding to use it before I took another dunking, I waved goodbye to
all h.

I got to the nemesis with no further problems.  I found him in the
centre of the level, seemingly obliviuos to the fact that he was
doomed.  Since he had the tsurugi I was afraid to fight him head-on, so
I sprayed him with bullets until he teleported away, kindly leaving the
blade on the floor.  The sad thing is that dual-wielding Excal+Grays
was more powerful than the tsurugi.  Still, it acted like a luckstone
so I carried it along with me.  Now that the nemesis could only fight
bare-handed, I swiftly found and dispatched him.  Hardly honourable,
but I doubt my samurai will have any trouble sleeping because of it.

By the time I got to Medusa, I had found and enchanted boots of speed,
gauntlets of dexterity, shimmering dragon scale mail, amulet of
reflection and a few other miscallaneous bits of kit.  I'd also gotten
my hands on a ring of conflict.  Since I don't like pets, I consider it
one of the best items in the game.

Medusa was simple, as always.  Then a couple of levels down I found the
castle.

I love the castle.  At this point I'd found a magic marker, and since I
had a lot of blank scrolls (see above) I put them to good use.  I
genocided L, ;, and puddings+slimes.  I had also genocided
disenchanters thanks to a throne.  So I just blasted the drawbridge
down, used a wand of cold to bridge the gap, and went in slicing and
dicing.  Soon I had loot a-plenty, including an assault rifle, and the
wand of wishing I'd been dying to get hold of.

The wand let me finish off my AK and enchant it as high as it would go.
 I got a helm of telepathy and a cloak of MR.  I wished for a new magic
marker and made good use of it.  Thanks to a trip to a armor shop I
soon had a fully enchanted AK.  Final AC of -37.

My wand had gone down to (0:0) at this point, so I paid a shopkeeper to
enchant it.  The cheeky lad took all my gold in addition to the asking
price!  The funny part is that I never made another wish with the wand,
although it's always good to have one around.

Up I travelled to say hi to Beholder and Vecna, before stealing their
body parts and keys.  That was easy.  The hand is a wonderful artifact.
 Regeneration and half phisical damage just for holding a rotting hand?
 I'll have some of that!

Just a quick mention:  i wandered into nightmare's realm, just to have
gotten everything, but couldn't find my way to him.  Oh well.

And with that sentiment, I hopped down into Gehennom.

At this point I would like to thank the person who had the wisdom to
shorten Gehennom.  I really mean it.  You've saved me so much cursing
and boredom.  If I ever meet you, drinks are on me.

All the demon-kings swiftly fell before my power.  At this point I was
virtually unstoppable.  Except for Demogorgon.  He nearly butchered me!
 It took several applications of unicorn horn and a dose of full
healing to save the day.  It's lucky that I'd just killed a cockatrice
in the previous level and was still toting its corpse.

After eviscerating Franky and his adorable monster - for kicks - I
found Vlad's tower, used the keys, and crushed the venerable vamp in
two attacks.  He really should be ashamed.

With candelabrum and bell in hand, I could proceed to the endgame:  a
duel with the wizard, which I've been fearing ever since his 16th
incarnation wiped the floor with me in Nethack.  It turned out that I
needn't have worried.  I'd been hoarding wands of death the entire game
and they made sushi out of him.  Add the book of the dead to my pile of
goodies!

So, invocation spoken, Moloch's sanctum opened up, and I proceeded to
carve my way through the undead, perform major surgery upon the priests
of moloch, and execute the high priest for the amulet.  Success!  But
the high priest was thougher than I remember.  Not quite as easy a
pushover that I thought it'd be.

I dashed up to the astral planes.  Rodney made a niusance of himself a
couple of times, but I used my faithful wands of death to sedate him.

The planes were easy.  I expected the air elementals to be nastily
though, but they were quite tolerable.  Finding the portals was a bit
of a problem (i only got 2 cursed scrolls of gold detection) but
through luck and perseverance I found my way to the Astral plane.

Now the last level was nice.  All those angels charging at me from
every direction was a treat.  I was worried about the riders, but I
kept them at bay.  Hacking my way through the multitudes, I found AO's
high altar, and the rest is history.



